This aircraft joins two GPL sources.

The flight dynamics model -- c172x.xml and the files it assembles, Engines/, Systems/ and
c172ap.xml -- is the "c172x" Cessna 172P Skyhawk II of the JSBSim project, by Tony Peden,
whose fileheader names the GNU General Public License. It is the stock model split into
files: the engine is given a name so a propeller can find it, and the stock CSV and socket
<output> and <input> blocks are left out.

  https://github.com/JSBSim-Team/jsbsim  (aircraft/c172x)

The meshes are the exterior and cabin of FlightGear's "c172p" (Models/c172-common.ac, the
panel, instruments, yokes, pedals and flap switch its Models/c172p.xml assembles, with their
textures), original by David Megginson, developed by the c172p team: gsagostinho,
wlbragg, Juanvvc, onox, thevirtualfer, Dany93, legoboyvdlp, dg-505, tigert, AndersG,
wkitty42, algefaen, Horacio, D-ECHO, Jarl Arntzen, Benedikt Hallinger, Sascha Reissner and
Josh Davidson. The model is cut into one glb per moving part, hinged where that model's
animations hinge it and stood on the flight model's gear contact points; the instruments
stand still at their rest reading. Parts FlightGear shows only for an option, a state or a
view -- the blurred propeller, cowl plugs, tie-downs, click hotspots, rain glass layers, lit
lamp states, wheel fairings, the glass-panel variant, damage, floats and skis -- are left out. The
model is distributed under the GNU General Public License v2.0 or later; see LICENSE in
this folder for its text.

  https://github.com/c172p-team/c172p

